hotspot: keep GC sources PIC on android

JvmOverrideFiles.gmk compiles the memory/oops/gc sources with -fno-PIC on
32-bit x86, an old pause-time optimisation (JDK-6454213). That cannot work on
android: shared objects must be position independent, and mixing -fno-PIC
objects with the PIC precompiled header stops the build outright:

  error: __PIC__ level differs in PCH file vs. current file

Skip the override on android, keyed on OPENJDK_TARGET_LIBC: configure derives
it from the target triple and only ever says gnu or musl for the linux x86
targets built here, while android (bionic) lands in "default". Termux deletes
the block outright (0020-x86-always-compile-with-PIC); gating it leaves the
linux/musl x86 builds with the optimisation they can still use.

diff --git a/make/hotspot/lib/JvmOverrideFiles.gmk b/make/hotspot/lib/JvmOverrideFiles.gmk
index 377018c..bb28cf2 100644
--- a/make/hotspot/lib/JvmOverrideFiles.gmk
+++ b/make/hotspot/lib/JvmOverrideFiles.gmk
@@ -62,7 +62,11 @@ ifeq ($(call isTargetOs, linux), true)
 	#
   endif
 
-  ifeq ($(call isTargetCpu, x86), true)
+  # Not on android: its shared objects must be position independent, and
+  # -fno-PIC here also collides with the PIC precompiled header
+  # ("__PIC__ level differs in PCH file vs. current file"). Only the gnu and
+  # musl x86 targets keep it; bionic reports its libc as "default".
+  ifeq ($(call isTargetCpu, x86)-$(if $(filter gnu musl, $(OPENJDK_TARGET_LIBC)),known,other), true-known)
     # Performance measurements show that by compiling GC related code, we could
     # significantly reduce the GC pause time on 32 bit Linux/Unix platforms by
     # compiling without the PIC flag (-fPIC on linux).
